Integrating gRPC Server Reflection with tonic-web in a Rust gRPC application enables web clients (like browsers) and tools (like Postman or grpcurl) to dynamically discover your API schema.For tips on setting up a gRPC server in Rust with reflection:19:08Tonic makes gRPC in Rust stupidly simpleDreams of CodeYouTube · Feb 22, 2024To get this working, you need to combine the tonic-reflection crate, tonic-web for browser compatibility, and serve it over a secure HTTPS (TLS) connection.1. Add Required DependenciesUpdate your Cargo.toml to include the required crates for web support and server reflection:toml[dependencies]
tonic = "0.12"
tonic-web = "0.12"
tonic-reflection = "0.12"
prost = "0.13"
tokio = { version = "1.0", features = ["full"] }
Use code with caution.2. Configure tonic-reflection & tonic-webWhen wiring up your server, you must register the reflection service alongside your actual gRPC services and wrap your router in the GrpcWebLayer.Here is how your server main.rs should look:rustuse tonic::transport::Server;
use tonic_web::GrpcWebLayer;

// Import your generated service definitions
pub mod my_service {
    tonic::include_proto!("myservice"); // replace with your proto package name
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let addr = "[::1]:50051".parse()?;

    // 1. Setup Reflection Service
    let reflection_service = tonic_reflection::server::Builder::configure()
        .register_encoded_file_descriptor_set(my_service::FILE_DESCRIPTOR_SET)
        .build_v1()?; // Or build_v1alpha() for older clients

    // 2. Add your actual service implementations
    let my_service_impl = my_service::MyServiceServer::new(...);

    // 3. Build the Server with tonic-web for gRPC-Web clients
    Server::builder()
        .accept_http2(true) // Required for tonic-web and native gRPC
        .layer(
            GrpcWebLayer::new() // Enables grpc-web compliance for web apps
        )
        .add_service(reflection_service)
        .add_service(my_service_impl)
        .serve_with_config(addr) // Or serve_with_tls() for HTTPS
        .await?;

    Ok(())
}
Use code with caution.3. Serving over HTTPSBrowsers and modern clients strongly prefer—and often require—HTTPS to establish gRPC-Web connections. While serve_with_config is great for local HTTP testing, for production HTTPS, you should configure your server using TLS:rustuse tonic::transport::{Server, Identity, ServerTlsConfig};

// Load your certificates
let cert = tokio::fs::read("cert.pem").await?;
let key = tokio::fs::read("key.pem").await?;
let identity = Identity::from_pem(cert, key);

Server::builder()
    .tls_config(ServerTlsConfig::new().identity(identity))?
    // ... add layers and services ...
    .serve_with_incoming(addr) // or use .serve() if a standard listener is configured
Use code with caution.4. Handling CORS (if applicable)If your gRPC-Web clients and the tonic backend are on different domains, the browser's security model might block the API requests. You can solve this by including a tower-http CORS layer before or after the GrpcWebLayer:rustuse tower_http::cors::{CorsLayer, Any};

let cors = CorsLayer::new()
    .allow_origin(Any) // Restrict this to your specific frontend URL in production
    .allow_headers(vec![hyper::header::CONTENT_TYPE, hyper::header::AUTHORIZATION])
    .allow_methods(vec![hyper::Method::POST, hyper::Method::OPTIONS]);

// Add this to your Server builder chain:
// .layer(cors)
// .layer(GrpcWebLayer::new())
Use code with caution.To help tailor this setup for your specific needs, let me know:Is your client a web application (gRPC-Web) or a backend service/CLI?Do you need assistance generating the file descriptor set from your .proto files?Are you using Axum to co-host HTTP routes alongside tonic?8 sitesgRPC-web CORS issue #1678 - GitHubApr 9, 2024 — Bug Report * Version. ❯ cargo tree | grep tonic │ │ │ │ └── tonic v0.11.0 │ │ │ └── tonic v0.11.0 (*) │ │ │ ├── tonic v0.11.0 (*) ...GitHubgrpc-rust/tonic-web/README.md at master - GitHubEnables tonic servers to handle requests from grpc-web clients directly, without the need of an external proxy.GitHubtonic_web - Rust - Docs.rsLimitations * tonic_web is designed to work with grpc-web-compliant clients only. It is not expected to handle arbitrary HTTP/x.x ...Docs.rsShow all

